When nothing in the page has the focus, Ladybird reports the AXWebArea
as AXFocused — exactly when it’s the page’s focused UI element. Chrome
follows the same rule: AXPlatformNodeCocoa’s isAccessibilityFocused
compares the node against GetFocus(), and BrowserAccessibilityManager::
GetFocusFromThisOrDescendantFrame() resolves that to the root when no
node in the page is focused. Gecko’s mozAccessible::moxFocused reads the
document’s FOCUSED state, which DocAccessible::NativeState() sets when
FocusMgr()->IsFocused() says so — and FocusManager::FocusedDOMNode()
falls back to the focused browsing context’s document when no element
has the focus. But Gecko also requires that browsing context to
hold the focus, so its web area isn’t focused while the URL bar is;
Ladybird, like Chrome, checks no keyboard focus. WebKit differs: Its
AccessibilityNodeObject::isFocused() returns false whenever the document
has no focused element, and reports a web area focused only while some
element has the focus and the frame’s selection is focused and active.
